# The ADK runtime on Nebius Token Factory (Nebius x NVIDIA hackathon, exploratory): the same studio as
# requirements.in, plus the one package ADK needs to reach a model that is not Gemini.
#
# **Its own venv, `python-nebius-adk/`, and its own lock, `requirements-nebius.txt`.** The Qloo entry runs on
# `python-adk/` from `requirements.txt`, and this must not move it: litellm brings a large transitive tree
# (openai, tokenizers, tiktoken, jsonschema...) that could otherwise shift a version the studio depends on.
# Delete the venv and these two files to revert.
#
# **Compiled against requirements.txt's pins**, so every package the two venvs share is at the Qloo lock's version
# and the studio runs on exactly what it runs on there; litellm adds 24 packages of its own and moves none. A plain
# compile drifts 34 shared packages to whatever is newest (protobuf 6 -> 7, google-genai 2.22 -> 2.31). To refresh:
# write requirements.txt's `name==version` lines to a constraints file (no hashes, no extras), then
#   uv pip compile src/adk_agent/requirements-nebius.in -c <constraints> --universal --python-version 3.13 \
#       --generate-hashes -o src/adk_agent/requirements-nebius.txt
-r requirements.in

# ADK's LiteLlm model (`google.adk.models.lite_llm`) imports it; LiteLLM's `nebius/` provider reads
# NEBIUS_API_KEY and speaks Token Factory's OpenAI-compatible API. The pattern is Nebius's own cookbook
# (token-factory-cookbook, agents/google-adk-tool-calling), which uses `LiteLlm(model="nebius/<org>/<model>")`.
#   litellm   https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm
#
# **Pinned for age, not newness.** 1.82.7 and 1.82.8 were a supply-chain compromise (2026-03-24; 1.82.8 ran a
# credential stealer from `litellm_init.pth` at every interpreter start). LiteLLM releases near daily, so this takes
# a patch release that has had time to be looked at: 1.104.2 (2026-10-08) over 1.106.0 (two days old at the time).
# The hashes in the lock hold it to exactly those files. After installing, check site-packages for `.pth` files.
litellm==1.104.2
